The Security Council,

Recalling its previous relevant resolutions, including resolutions 825 (1993), 1540 (2004), 1695 (2006), 1718 (2006), 1874 (2009), 1887 (2009), 1928 (2010), 1985 (2011), 2050 (2012), 2087 (2013), 2094 (2013), 2141 (2014), 2207 (2015), 2270 (2016), 2276 (2016), 2321 (2016), 2345 (2017), 2356 (2017), 2371 (2017), 2375 (2017), 2397 (2017), 2407 (2018), 2464 (2019), 2515 (2020), 2569 (2021), and 2627 (2022),

Recalling the statements of its President of 6 October 2006 (S/PRST/2006/41), 13 April 2009 (S/PRST/2009/7), 16 April 2012 (S/PRST/2012/13), and 29 August 2017 (S/PRST/2017/16),

Recalling the 2018 inter-Korean summit and the Singapore Joint Statement of 12 June 2018, and the commitment to establish a lasting and stable peace regime on the Korean Peninsula and to achieve the complete denuclearization of the Korean Peninsula,

Deeply concerned by the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea’s continued development and testing of ballistic missiles, including the launch of an intercontinental ballistic missile on 25 May 2022 and other recent launches, in violation of relevant Security Council resolutions,

Condemning in the strongest terms the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea’s ballistic missile launches, which are contributing to the development of its weapons of mass destruction and ballistic missile programmes and increasing regional and global tensions,

Reaffirming that the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea must abandon all nuclear weapons and existing nuclear programmes in a complete, verifiable and irreversible manner, and immediately cease all related activities, including the reconstruction of the Punggye-ri nuclear test site,

Noting with grave concern the humanitarian situation in the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea, including the recent outbreak of COVID-19, and emphasizing that the measures imposed by the Security Council are not intended to have adverse humanitarian consequences for the civilian population,

Reaffirming the importance of the full and effective implementation of the measures imposed by the relevant Security Council resolutions by all Member States, and stressing that such implementation is essential to achieve the complete denuclearization of the Korean Peninsula,

Determining that the proliferation of nuclear, chemical and biological weapons, as well as their means of delivery, continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Condemns in the strongest terms the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea’s launch of an intercontinental ballistic missile on 25 May 2022 and other ballistic missile launches, and demands that the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea immediately cease all ballistic missile launches and refrain from any further launches, nuclear tests or other destabilizing actions;

2. Demands that the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea immediately abandon all nuclear weapons and existing nuclear programmes in a complete, verifiable and irreversible manner, cease all related activities, and fully comply with its obligations under the relevant Security Council resolutions;

3. Reaffirms that the measures imposed by resolutions 1718 (2006), 1874 (2009), 2087 (2013), 2094 (2013), 2270 (2016), 2321 (2016), 2356 (2017), 2371 (2017), 2375 (2017), and 2397 (2017) shall remain in force, and calls upon all Member States to fully and effectively implement those measures;

4. Underlines that it will continue to review the situation and is prepared to take further significant measures in the event of additional Democratic People’s Republic of Korea intercontinental ballistic missile launches or nuclear tests;

5. Requests the Panel of Experts, established pursuant to resolution 1874 (2009), to include in its regular reports to the Committee established pursuant to resolution 1718 (2006) an assessment of the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea’s compliance with this resolution and the implementation of the measures reaffirmed in paragraph 3 above, and to provide recommendations;

6. Reaffirms that the measures imposed by the relevant Security Council resolutions are not intended to have adverse humanitarian consequences for the civilian population of the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea, and calls for the full and expedited processing of humanitarian exemptions, including for COVID-19-related assistance and medical supplies;

7. Urges the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea to respond positively to offers of dialogue and diplomacy, including from the United States of America and the Republic of Korea, and to return to denuclearization negotiations;

8. Calls upon all Member States and relevant international organizations to provide humanitarian assistance to the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea, particularly in response to the COVID-19 outbreak, consistent with the relevant Security Council resolutions;

9. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

The Security Council,

