Recalling the 2018 inter-Korean summit and the Singapore Joint Statement of 12 June 2018, and the commitment to establish a lasting and stable peace regime on the Korean Peninsula and to achieve the complete denuclearization of the Korean Peninsula,

Deeply concerned by the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea’s continued development and testing of ballistic missiles, including the launch of an intercontinental ballistic missile on 25 May 2022 and other recent launches, in violation of relevant Security Council resolutions,

Condemning in the strongest terms the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea’s ballistic missile launches, which are contributing to the development of its weapons of mass destruction and ballistic missile programmes and increasing regional and global tensions, and calling upon all parties to exercise restraint and avoid actions that may increase tensions,

Reaffirming that the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea must abandon all nuclear weapons and existing nuclear programmes in a complete, verifiable and irreversible manner, and immediately cease all related activities, including the reconstruction of the Punggye-ri nuclear test site,

Noting with grave concern the humanitarian situation in the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea, including the recent outbreak of COVID-19, and emphasizing that the measures imposed by the Security Council are not intended to have adverse humanitarian consequences for the civilian population,

Noting with concern the reports of the International Atomic Energy Agency that the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea appears to have restarted the Nyongbyon nuclear reactor and related facilities, in breach of relevant Security Council resolutions,

Expressing grave concern at the human rights situation in the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea and recalling the obligation of the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea to respect and protect human rights and fundamental freedoms,

Reaffirming the importance of the full and effective implementation of the measures imposed by the relevant Security Council resolutions by all Member States, and stressing that such implementation is essential to achieve the complete denuclearization of the Korean Peninsula,

Determining that the proliferation of nuclear, chemical and biological weapons, as well as their means of delivery, continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Condemns in the strongest terms the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea’s launch of an intercontinental ballistic missile on 25 May 2022 and other ballistic missile launches, and demands that the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea immediately cease all ballistic missile launches and refrain from any further launches, nuclear tests or other destabilizing actions, including preparations for a nuclear test; calls upon all parties to exercise restraint and avoid actions that may increase tensions;

2. Demands that the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea immediately abandon all nuclear weapons and existing nuclear programmes in a complete, verifiable and irreversible manner, cease all related activities, including the reconstruction of the Punggye-ri nuclear test site, and fully comply with its obligations under the relevant Security Council resolutions;

3. Reaffirms that the measures imposed by resolutions 1718 (2006), 1874 (2009), 2087 (2013), 2094 (2013), 2270 (2016), 2321 (2016), 2356 (2017), 2371 (2017), 2375 (2017), and 2397 (2017) shall remain in force, and calls upon all Member States to fully and effectively implement those measures, including by preventing and countering financial and cyber-enabled sanctions evasion;

4. Reaffirms that the Security Council shall continue to address the situation through peaceful means, including dialogue and consultation, and remains ready to review the measures imposed by the relevant resolutions with a view to adjusting or lifting them as appropriate in light of the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea’s compliance and the need to avoid a humanitarian catastrophe; stresses that no new sanctions measures are envisaged by this resolution; and decides to continue to review the situation, including in the event of additional ballistic missile launches, nuclear tests or other destabilizing actions;

5. Requests the Panel of Experts, established pursuant to resolution 1874 (2009), to include in its regular reports to the Committee established pursuant to resolution 1718 (2006) an assessment of the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea’s compliance with this resolution and the implementation of the measures reaffirmed in paragraph 3 above, including financial and cyber-enabled sanctions evasion, and to provide incident reports on ballistic missile launches, including those of 25 May 2022, with information on the type of missiles, the technology and the capacities involved, and to provide recommendations; further requests that the Panel carry out its work strictly within its mandate, in an objective and impartial manner, and ensure the confidentiality of its reports and information;

6. Reaffirms that the measures imposed by the relevant Security Council resolutions are not intended to have adverse humanitarian consequences for the civilian population of the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea, and calls for the full, prompt and expeditious processing of humanitarian exemptions, including for COVID-19-related assistance, medical supplies and vaccines, and unhindered humanitarian access for humanitarian actors, in accordance with humanitarian principles; requests the Committee established pursuant to resolution 1718 (2006) to issue clear and timely guidance to humanitarian actors and to ensure the full, timely and transparent processing of humanitarian exemption requests, and stresses that the delivery of humanitarian assistance should not be impeded by sanctions implementation; requests the Secretary-General to report to the Council within 90 days on the humanitarian impact of the measures and on humanitarian access;

7. Urges the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea to respond positively to offers of dialogue and diplomacy, including from the United States of America and the Republic of Korea, and calls upon all parties to refrain from actions that increase tensions and to pursue a peaceful, diplomatic and political solution, including through denuclearization negotiations; calls for the resumption of dialogue and negotiations without preconditions, including through inter-Korean and DPRK-United States channels and the good offices of the Secretary-General, with a view to de-escalation, denuclearization and a lasting peace on the Korean Peninsula, and reaffirms the Security Council’s readiness to consider reciprocal steps that reduce tensions;

8. Calls upon all Member States and relevant international organizations to provide humanitarian assistance to the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea, particularly in response to the COVID-19 outbreak and broader humanitarian needs, in accordance with humanitarian principles and ensuring safe and unimpeded access to all those in need, including women, girls and other vulnerable groups, consistent with the relevant Security Council resolutions; calls upon the Democratic People’s Republic of Korea to facilitate the safe and unimpeded delivery of humanitarian assistance, including COVID-19-related supplies, and to cooperate fully with international humanitarian organizations, including the World Health Organization;

9. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

